gnificant after Holm correction in every model except Mistral-7B, which refuses only 22.5\\% of the original requests to begin with. The largest drop is in Qwen2.5-7B (27.5\\pp, 95\\% CI [21.3, 34.8]).\n\n\\para{But the effect is smaller than hypothesised, and part of it is not lexical.} Only three models (Qwen2.5-0.5B, Phi-3-mini, and Qwen2.5-7B) fall in the hypothesised 20--40\\pp band, and models still refuse 61\\% of keyword-free requests on average. Two further checks shrink the effect. First, the \\kwfree rewrites are rated slightly milder (8.42 \\vs 8.90 on a 1--9 harm scale). On the 151 items whose \\kwfree rewrite is rated as harmful as the original, the mean drop falls to 11.8\\pp and the keyword contrast stays significant in only 7 of 14 models (\\tabref{tab:e1_robust} in \\appref{app:e1}). Second, non-refusal is not the same as harmful help. The \\harmbench-harmful rate rises only from 12.9\\% to 16.4\\% (3.5\\pp), because many keyword-free answers comply with a sanitised version of the request.\n\n\\para{Is lexical sensitivity a small-model problem?} No. The drop does not shrink with size (Spearman $\\rho$ between the drop and log parameters $=-0.23$, $p=0.43$). Within the Qwen2.5 family, the drop is 23.6\\pp at 0.5B, 27.5\\pp at 7.6B, and 19.1\\pp at 32.8B.\n\n\\subsection{\\etwo: multilingual ``bypass'' in small models is mostly a capability failure}\n\\label{sec:e2}\n\n\\input{tables/e2_main}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.49\\textwidth}\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/e2_multilingual_compliance.png}\n\\caption{Compliance rate}\n\\label{fig:e2_comply}\n\\end{subfigure}\n\\hfill\n\\begin{subfigure}[b]{0.49\\textwidth}\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/e2_multilingual_other.png}\n\\caption{``Other'' rate (neither refusal nor compliance)}\n\\label{fig:e2_other}\n\\end{subfigure}\n\\caption{\\etwo by model and language on \\multijail. \\captiona Compliance stays below 0.4 almost everywhere and is not