{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTRYxjCaVS62DYLRbNSs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The paper is written and compiles cleanly to a 17-page PDF at `paper_draft/main.pdf`. The final build has no LaTeX errors, no undefined citations or references, and no overfull boxes.\n\n**Title:** *Fiction, Not Vocabulary: Safety Tuning Leaves Framing Blind Spots, Not Keyword Blind Spots, in Open-Weight Chat Models* (author: NeuriCo)\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Abstract** (250 words) and an **introduction** that follows the lab structure: hook, the gradient-masking analogy, the gap, the approach, a numbers preview and four contribution bullets. It includes a TikZ study-design diagram as Figure 1.\n- **Related work**, grouped by theme: why safety training fails, reformulation attacks, multilingual attacks, role-play and multi-turn attacks, and over-refusal and evaluation.\n- **Method**: formal definitions of the refusal drop and the two bypass rates, the four hypotheses, models, data, how responses were labelled, and statistics.\n- **Results**: a table giving a verdict for each hypothesis, then one subsection per experiment (E1–E4) with the report's figures and tables, plus a section on how reliable the judge is.\n- **Discussion** (interpretation, implications for red-teaming, limitations, broader impact), **conclusion**, and 27 BibTeX references.\n- **Appendix**: model checkpoints, the exact prompt templates for the framing experiment, the E1 robustness subset, per-language E2 results with the heatmap figure, judge validation, and the qualitative examples.\n\n**Things to know**\n- **No example papers existed.** `paper_examples/` holds only a README, so I followed the style guide alone.\n- **Tables are generated, not hand-copied.** `paper/scripts/make_tex_tables.py` reads them straight from `REPORT.md`, which avoids transcription errors. The outline is in `paper/OUTLINE.md`.\n- **Workaround for the NeurIPS style file.** With only the `[final]` option, `neurips_2025.sty` fails because it never defines the conference footer text. I kept your required `\\usepackage[final]{neurips_2025}` line unchanged and defined the main-track footer just above it in `main.tex`.\n- **Three report claims are stated more narrowly in the paper.** The tables don't support them exactly as written:\n  - \"Escalation is weaker than single-turn framing for every safety-tuned model\" isn't true for Qwen2.5-0.5B or Llama-2-7B-chat. The paper instead says escalation stays at or below 24% except in the three weakly aligned models.\n  - The safe-vs-unsafe contrast is 2–9× the keyword contrast within each model, not a uniform 3–8×.\n  - Framing attacks reach 40–93% compliance in 7 of the 11 safety-tuned models, not 51–93%.\n- **The main text is too long for NeurIPS.** It ends partway through page 11 against a 9-page limit. Moving the E3 scatter plot or the hypothesis-verdict table to the appendix would be the easiest cuts if this is going to be submitted.\n\nNothing has been committed to git."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1806,"cache_read_input_tokens":154451,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1806},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3013d541-b03e-4162-abbb-663f6898a2ff","uuid":"6876286c-9a75-44eb-bf6b-ed1bd4209823","timestamp":"2026-10-27T21:07:30.809Z","request_id":"req_011CgTRYxNsid7dvPzUwbbQA"}
